{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas` skill MUST be the home of routing tasks to skills, `habeas status` and `habeas info`, directive and reference syntax, distributed requirements, and gotchas common to every task.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The subject says the `habeas` skill MUST be the home of task routing, `habeas status` and `habeas info`, directive and reference syntax, distributed requirements, and gotchas common to every task. Every clause is about what the skill documents say and where they say it. None of them prescribes a library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or delegation through a named interface. The skill is Markdown, so no code structure is involved.\n\nI also considered the parent clause that each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill, and the clause that a skill is its `SKILL.md` plus its `references/` files. Both are content-placement rules. A skill that put the same content in other files in a different arrangement would still satisfy them, as long as the habeas skill is the one home for these topics. So the placement is discretionary and carries no structural obligation that needs implementation evidence.\n\nThe supplied roots do contain the topics:\n- `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md#section[Habeas]/section[Workflow]` has the task-to-skill routing table.\n- `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md#section[Habeas]/section[Inspecting]` covers inspection.\n- `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md#section[Habeas]/section[Requirements,%20examples,%20and%20definitions]` and `#section[Habeas]/section[References]` cover directive and reference syntax.\n- `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md#section[Habeas]/section[Gotchas]` has the gotchas.\n- `assets/skills/habeas/references/syntax-details.md` holds the syntax details.\n- `assets/skills/habeas/references/distributed-requirements.md` covers distributed requirements.\n\nThe tool returned most section bodies elided, so I could check headings and structure, not the full prose. I did not confirm from the text that `habeas status` and `habeas info` are covered, or that each topic appears in only one skill. That part belongs to the behavioral and content review, not to structural evidence.\n\nbehavioral: The subject requires that the `habeas` skill be the home of five topics: routing, `habeas status` and `habeas info`, directive and reference syntax, distributed requirements, and shared gotchas. The cited parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure` adds that each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_habeas_skill_is_home_of_routing_inspection_syntax_and_shared_gotchas]` covers each topic:\n- **Routing:** it asserts the `| Task | Skill |` table in the `## Workflow` section.\n- **Inspection:** it asserts `habeas status [<selection>]` and `habeas info <selection>` in `## Inspecting`.\n- **Directive syntax:** it asserts the `{req}`, `{example}` and `{definition}` descriptions in `## Requirements, examples, and definitions`.\n- **Reference syntax:** it asserts the reference-writing and fencing phrases in `## References`.\n- **Gotchas:** it asserts the working-directory discovery phrase in `## Gotchas`.\n- **Distributed requirements:** it asserts the `# Distributed requirements` heading in `references/distributed-requirements.md`. `assert_home` also asserts the home marker \"Each site-query match makes a site\" in the habeas skill.\n- **Syntax details:** it asserts both headings in `references/syntax-details.md`.\n\nThe `assert_home(\"habeas\")` helper adds the home-exclusivity check. It asserts that every habeas marker from `home_markers` appears in the habeas skill and in none of the other four skills in `SKILLS`. Its `section` and `assert_text` helpers do real containment assertions.\n\nThe markers span all five topics, plus the CI gotcha text. A violation would be caught if a topic's content were missing from habeas or restated in another skill. I found no unmarked relevant test and no unchecked obligation.",
